"Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier European AI startup Mistral has raised €3 billion in a Series D funding round led by Samsung Electronics, achieving a valuation exceeding €21 billion. Mistral secured €3 billion in Series D funding at a valuation exceeding €21 billion, marking the largest equity investment round in the history of European tech companies. The funding round was led by Samsung Electronics, with participation from Scaleup Europe Fund, PSG Equity, and others; the capital raised will be used for frontier research, expanding computing infrastructure, and global business expansion. Mistral provides a full stack, including open-weight models and infrastructure, enabling enterprises and governments to preserve data sovereignty and deploy AI without vendor lock-in. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral AI partner to deliver specialized sovereign AI intelligence to enterprise data across on-premises and hybrid environments. Integrates Mistral's AI models into the Cloudera hybrid data platform to support inference and deployment across public/private clouds, on-premises, and air-gapped environments Enables enterprises to leverage their proprietary data to train and build customized AI models while maintaining complete ownership of both data and models Addresses the demand for Sovereign AI, guaranteeing customers full control over their data, intelligence, compute, and overall operations Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Introduces a case study of successfully modernizing a large-scale Fortran 77 legacy scientific codebase lacking tests and documentation into modern C++ using Mistral's AI agents. Successfully transformed legacy code for a physics-intensive reservoir simulator from a European energy enterprise into a modern C++ object-oriented architecture Went beyond simple syntax translation by building a parity harness to verify numerical equivalence and performing agent-driven pre-documentation Ensured code quality and maintainability through a structured workflow combining agent autonomy with review and oversight from human engineers Mistral x HUMAIN Mistral and HUMAIN have entered into a strategic partnership valued in the hundreds of millions of Euros to strengthen sovereign AI capabilities in Saudi Arabia and the Middle East. Mistral and HUMAIN announced a strategic collaboration spanning AI infrastructure, advanced model development, and AI solution deployment across Saudi Arabia and the Middle East. Initial efforts will focus on developing and localizing frontier models with outstanding Arabic language performance, as well as specialized models in cybersecurity and speech. They are pursuing a joint go-to-market strategy for sovereign AI that leverages HUMAIN's data center infrastructure and guarantees data and computing control for regulated industries, such as finance, manufacturing, telecommunications, and the public sector. Agentic Search. More accurate and efficient results from your AI systems. Mistral has announced Agentic Search, a search layer that retrieves, explores, and verifies information within complex and sensitive documents through a multi-step loop, improving accuracy while reducing latency and token usage. Navigates and verifies complex, long documents and multiple sources based on existing indexes using five tools: search, open, navigate, read, and grep Supports secure access to sensitive domain-specific data without crossing cloud or on-premises isolation boundaries Delivered via the Mistral Search Toolkit as well as Libraries in Studio and Vibe, significantly improving accuracy while reducing latency and token consumption DiscoSign: Discourse-Aware Text to Sign Language Gloss Translation This study introduces DiscoSign, an LLM-based framework that translates text into sign language gloss by incorporating discourse-level linguistic characteristics. Proposed DiscoSign, a discourse-aware translation framework, to overcome the limitations of conventional sentence-level sign language translation. Addressed three key discourse phenomena—spatial coreference resolution, Question-Answer Clauses (QACs), and concept-gloss consistency—using a modular LLM-based approach. Introduced new evaluation metrics to assess discourse coherence, demonstrating significant improvements in spatial consistency and entity tracking performance through experiments. Putting Captions to the Test: Evaluating Video Caption Quality through Multiple-Choice Question Answering This study proposes CapQuiz, a novel benchmark, and CapF1, a composite evaluation metric, designed to evaluate video caption quality based on informational fidelity and factuality through multiple-choice question answering. It points out the limitations of existing video captioning evaluation metrics that unfairly penalize diverse valid expressions due to over-reliance on simple lexical overlap with ground-truth reference texts. It introduces CapQuiz, a human-verified reference-free evaluation benchmark based on multiple-choice question answering, covering 24 domains and 10 question types. It proposes the CapF1 metric, which combines CapP (measuring factuality) and CapR (measuring information coverage), demonstrating a high correlation with human evaluation. SimpleDesign: A Joint Model for Protein Sequence and Structure Codesign Research on SimpleDesign, a generative model that directly co-designs protein sequences and 3D structures end-to-end in data space without latent space compression. Instead of autoencoder-based multi-stage latent space training, it uses a single-stage end-to-end objective combining sequence cross-entropy and structure regression loss. It adopts a Transformer-based multimodal backbone architecture that simultaneously supports modality-specific processing and global self-attention. Trained on over 2 million sequence-structure pair data, it demonstrated outstanding performance across benchmarks for co-design and unconditional sequence and structure generation. Palantir Foundry and cuOpt drive NVIDIA supply chain allocation NVIDIA combines and applies Palantir Foundry, its cuOpt optimization library, and Nemotron models to automate complex global hardware supply chain allocation and decision-making. Built a digital supply chain intelligence system combining Palantir Foundry's ontology with cuOpt mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) to support Grace Blackwell NVL72 and the upcoming Vera Rubin architecture. Fine-tuned and deployed Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, a 30B MoE model, via the NeMo pipeline to process unstructured data such as supplier call logs, weather, and geopolitical risks, in addition to quantitative optimization. The post-trained Nemotron 3.5 Lightning achieved 86.7% decision accuracy in evaluations on historical allocation records, significantly outperforming large base models. Meta says it's changing AI suggestions after posing invasive personal questions Meta has updated Meta AI's suggested prompt feature following controversy over prompts asking for excessive personal information, such as the identity of a user's child and their place of residence. A video went viral showing Meta AI suggesting an invasive prompt asking 'Who is the child passenger?' below a Facebook video posted by Instagram user Kalie Robins. Meta AI synthesized past posts and relatives' updates to inquire about the child's age and residence, and even surfaced a photo the user claimed to have deleted years ago. A Meta spokesperson acknowledged that the feature missed the mark and stated that they have resolved issues with prompt suggestions related to personal topics. Cohere Releases North Small Translate: A 218B MoE Translation Model That Scores 83.6 on WMT26 Across 50 Languages Cohere has released 'North Small Translate', an open-weight 218B MoE machine translation model that supports 50 languages and achieved high scores in WMT26 evaluations. It supports translation across 50 languages using a sparse MoE architecture where only 25B out of the 218B total parameters are activated per token. It scored 83.6 in its WMT26 evaluation, outperforming existing models including DeepL and Google Translate. It provides a self-error-correcting Agentic variant model, distributed via free API calls, non-commercial self-hosting, and commercial licensing. Sakana AI Launches Fugu Max and Fugu Ultra v2 for Cheaper, Stronger Multi-Agent Orchestration Sakana AI has launched multi-agent orchestration models Fugu Max and Fugu Ultra v2, maximizing cost efficiency and advanced complex reasoning performance, respectively. Rather than a single foundation model, Fugu is a trained orchestrator architecture that routes tasks to the optimal model behind a single API. Fugu Max leverages a diverse pool of open-source and specialized models, including NVIDIA Nemotron, to deliver high performance at up to 40-60% lower costs compared to major competitors. Targeting complex reasoning, autonomous research, and software engineering, Fugu Ultra v2 significantly enhances performance on visual and structured data reasoning as well as coding benchmarks. Google Research Releases ToolGrad: Answer-First Framework Hits 99.8% Pass Rate for Tool-Use Data Generation Researchers from Google and other institutions have unveiled the ToolGrad framework, which achieves a 99.8% success rate in data generation by first generating verified tool-use chains and reverse-engineering user queries. To address computational waste caused by exploration failures in conventional query-first approaches, the framework introduces a reverse pipeline that executes actual APIs to construct ground-truth chains first before generating queries. Through cyclic iterations across four modules—API Proposer, API Executors, API Selector, and LLM Updater—it efficiently generates verified tool-use workflows and highly consistent synthetic query data. Gemma-3 models fine-tuned on 500 samples generated by ToolGrad (ToolGrad-500) achieved performance rivaling top proprietary models on the Berkeley Function Calling Leaderboard, with code and model weights released as open source. From Spaghetti Code to Clean Python: A Beginner’s Guide A beginner's guide introducing how to refactor tangled, spaghetti Python code into clean, maintainable functions following the Single Responsibility Principle. When too many responsibilities are concentrated in a single function, dependencies become unclear and logic errors are prone to occur; therefore, they should be separated into independent functions with clear inputs and outputs. Diagnoses bugs dependent on execution order and issues with shared state mutation through an order processing example that tangles discount calculation, inventory updates, and email dispatching together. Presents techniques such as modeling using dataclasses instead of dictionaries, raising proper exceptions instead of silent warning prints, and unit testing independent functions. 5 Python Techniques for Efficient Resource Orchestration An article discussing resource orchestration techniques based on the Python standard library to efficiently manage limited resources in concurrent environments. Production-grade resource orchestration that safely manages limited resources beyond simple parallel I/O processing is essential. Leverages modern standard libraries, including Python 3.11+ for core techniques and thread-safety improvements in Python 3.14. Resolves orphan task leak issues inherent to asyncio.gather by utilizing asyncio.TaskGroup, which provides structured concurrency. OpenDiscoveryTrace: Process Traces for Evaluating AI Scientist Workflows Moving away from traditional methods that only evaluate final outputs, this paper introduces OpenDiscoveryTrace, an open dataset designed to record and evaluate the full reasoning and task execution process of AI scientist agents step by step. Structured into 9 fields, it records 558 complete agent execution trajectories across 124 scientific tasks—including drug discovery, materials science, genomics, and literature analysis—performed by 7 models. While the final success rates among frontier models were comparable at 84-89%, process tracing revealed qualitative behavioral differences obscured by outcome-oriented evaluations, such as Claude Opus 4.6 generating 30 times more errors per trajectory than GPT-5.4. To support process-level evaluation, scientific agent auditing, and AI governance research, the dataset, trace schema, agent harness, and benchmark definitions are released under the CC BY 4.0 license. Adaptive Entangled Game Modules in Artificial General Intelligence A study presenting the necessity of integrating adaptive entangled game modules into AGI architecture by explaining the collective behaviors of interacting agents through a probability wave model Introduced Generalized Behavioral Intelligence (GBI) non-local probability wave equations to derive collective behaviors and verifiable eigenmodes of interacting adaptive agents Empirical analysis of intraday trading data from the Chinese stock market revealed that adaptive entangled game modes explain 82-94% (89% overall) of all decision patterns, providing empirical support for the brain neural fiber entanglement hypothesis (LCA hypothesis) Proposed the development of compact and efficient Humanoid Processing Unit (HPU)-based AGI by combining probability wave-based entangled brain simulations to overcome the limitations of conventional artificial neural networks that rely on trillions of opaque parameters Subagents vs Agent Skills: Executing Reusable Knowledge for Long-Horizon Agentic Tasks This study analyzes the effectiveness and trade-offs of executing reusable knowledge via subagents with independent contexts, rather than relying on traditional agent skill approaches that directly load instructions into the main context during long-horizon tasks. Traditional agent skill approaches accumulate instructions directly into the main context, leading to degraded reasoning performance as tasks grow longer. When input/output contracts are clear and procedural knowledge is well-defined, executing via subagents with isolated contexts outperforms traditional skill execution. While the subagent approach delivers improved performance, it introduces a trade-off of additional token consumption (communication overhead) required for coordination between the main agent and subagents. Gradland: On Phenomenal Experience, Differentiated Across Many Dimensions A study exploring the hypothesis in an artificial neural network setting that gradients or Jacobians, the first-order structures of physical interactions, define the structure of phenomenal conscious experience. Constructed 'Gradland', a hypothetical artificial neural network environment where physical laws are known and mostly differentiable, to model phenomenal experience Introduced two structural metrics for Jacobians: effective rank and cohesion, based on Kirchhoff complexity Explained seven phenomena, including the duration of experience, the distinction between vividness and ambiguity, texture perception, newborn sensory confusion, the distinction between clarity and obscurity of ideas, the feeling of learning processes, and the function of rich, dense experiences Decision-Focused Active Learning for Scale-Aware Critical-Materials Recovery This study applies decision-focused active learning techniques to reduce the number of experiments and make optimal process decisions during the scale-up of critical materials recovery processes. Based on records from the Pacific Northwest National Laboratory (PNNL) autonomous selective precipitation workflow CICERO, active learning was applied to achieve maximum enrichment with fewer experiments than conventional non-adaptive space-filling methods. In the benchmark for recycling spent NdFeB magnets, adaptive policies reached the maximum enrichment result—which originally required 48 experiments—in just 16 to 24 wells (experiments). A method for selecting experimental batches based on downstream Bayes risk reduction rate was proposed to minimize economic impact and process decision losses. M3-Former: Multimodal Transformer with Mixture-of-Experts for Long-Term Vessel Trajectory Prediction This paper proposes the M3-Former framework, which combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture to enhance the performance of long-term vessel trajectory prediction. A pretrained LLM is used to align semantic information, such as static vessel attributes and navigation intentions, with dynamic trajectory features to build a unified multimodal representation. A dual-granularity MoE architecture is introduced, comprising sequence-level experts that model global route trends and token-level experts that refine detailed maneuvering behaviors. A Steering-Weighted Cross-Entropy loss function is designed to alleviate the long-tail distribution problem of turning samples. Halo: Improving forecast accuracy through heteroscedastic estimation A study on the Halo methodology demonstrating that heteroscedastic estimation techniques, previously used for uncertainty estimation in time series forecasting, also improve the accuracy of point forecasts. Proposed the Halo technique, which reuses existing deep time-series forecasting model architectures by adding an auxiliary output to estimate distribution scale and training with negative log-likelihood (NLL). Applied to three state-of-the-art models across five markets in a standard electricity price forecasting benchmark, improving MSE and MAE performance in 28 out of 30 comparisons. Confirmed that the act of estimating scale itself matters more than the specific scale estimation architecture (auxiliary head or parallel network), and existing hyperparameters optimized for point forecasting can be used directly without retuning. Zero-shot rib design: merging training-free generative prior with topology optimization This study proposes a zero-shot rib design framework that combines generative priors from pre-trained text-to-image diffusion models with density-based topology optimization to reflect engineers' natural language prompt intents and enhance structural performance. Utilizes a frozen text-to-image diffusion model without additional training as a source of design knowledge, integrating it into the physics computation loop of topology optimization via Score Distillation Sampling (SDS). Combines generative gradients guided by natural language prompts with finite element sensitivities at each iteration, ensuring only geometric features adhering to physical laws survive. The generative model's prior suppresses dead-end branches in the rib skeleton, achieving a significant compliance reduction compared to conventional gradient-based baselines. Artificial Intelligence Algorithms for the Detection of Pathologies Related to Lung Cancer through Image Analysis using Convolutional Neural Networks and Data Augmentation: a systematic mapping of the literature A systematic literature review and mapping study on research trends in AI-based image analysis for early detection of lung cancer utilizing convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and data augmentation techniques. Analyzed 96 papers published from 2015 to the present, selected from academic databases including PubMed, IEEE Xplore, Scopus, and Web of Science. Convolutional neural network (CNN) techniques combining transfer learning and data augmentation emerged as key promising technologies to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of image interpretation. While demonstrating high sensitivity and specificity, overcoming limitations for clinical translation is required, such as a lack of standardized data, model explainability, patient privacy, and ethical challenges. GEOSTEER: Geodesic Optimization for Activation Steering in Large Language Models Proposes GeoSteer, a novel methodology that enhances control performance by steering activation vectors in large language models (LLMs) across multiple steps via norm preservation and Riemannian geometric optimization. Existing norm-preserving activation steering techniques relied on predefined single-step updates, limiting their ability to capture the complex structure of activation distributions. GeoSteer frames activation steering as a Riemannian optimization problem, updating activations along the representation manifold through a sequence of small geodesic steps. By learning a nonlinear activation space objective function to distinguish target from non-target activations, each steering step is adaptively controlled, producing smoother and more stable outcomes. NCP-ArchPreview Technical Report: Moving towards Latent Space Language Models through Next Concept Prediction A research report proposing NCP-ArchPreview, a latent space language model that goes beyond traditional Next Token Prediction (NTP) to predict discrete concepts spanning multiple tokens. Introduces an architecture that constructs a product-quantized concept vocabulary from hidden states and predicts future concepts (NCP) via a dedicated concept module to inform token generation. Demonstrates the largest-scale empirical validation among latent space language models by training an 8.9B-parameter model on 5.73T tokens of the Dolma-3 dataset. Proves high efficiency by reaching the same pre-training loss as OLMo-3-7B using only 51.3% of the tokens, improving the downstream macro average score by 2.45 points, and providing a lightweight interface for domain adaptation. CMNIE: An Information Extraction Benchmark for Chinese Military News This study proposes CMNIE, an information extraction benchmark dataset designed to jointly extract events, arguments, entities, and relations from Chinese military news. Constructed CMNIE, an integrated military domain information extraction benchmark consisting of 13,000 instances collected from open Chinese military news Provided manual annotations for 7 event types, 10 argument roles, 7 entity types, and 8 relation types Evaluation results of supervised models and large language models (LLMs) demonstrated high difficulty in relation extraction and exact event-argument span matching, with zero-shot LLMs frequently failing at boundary extraction Think Before You Link: Rarity, Reasoning, and Retrieval in Multilingual Entity Linking This study redefines rare entities based on knowledge graph structures in multilingual and multimodal entity linking, significantly improving rare entity linking performance by combining iterative retrieval with reasoning. Utilized structural connectivity metrics of knowledge graphs instead of simple popularity metrics such as page views to identify previously overlooked rare entities and characterize various failure types. Proposed a training-free framework where reasoning-capable vision-language models iteratively explore Wikipedia and reason over findings, demonstrating complementary synergy between reasoning and retrieval. Achieved a 6.9% overall improvement and up to a 23.3% gain on rare-entity slices over the state of the art on MERLIN, a 5-language benchmark, and released MERLIN-Rare for evaluation. Multilingual in Name Only? Cultural and Linguistic Weaknesses of LLMs in Urdu A research finding showing that multilingual large language models (LLMs) exhibit severe limitations in terms of grammar, semantics, and cultural depth in low-resource languages such as Urdu. Using Urdu as a representative low-resource language, the study generated and analyzed a corpus of 93 stories (Urdu-Stories) using three contemporary LLMs (GPT-5.1, Qwen-3-Max, and DeepSeek-3.1). The generated stories frequently suffered from basic grammatical and semantic errors, lacked coherence, contained unnatural repetitions, and exhibited pervasive cultural superficiality. Even with few-shot prompting applied, most cultural and contextual errors remained unresolved, confirming limitations in the reliability of LLMs for low-resource languages. Larger Context Window, Fewer Overcorrections: Optimizing Prompts and Batching for Minimal-Edit Grammatical Error Correction This study proposes prompt optimization and batching methodologies to reduce overcorrections and achieve performance comparable to fine-tuned models in Large Language Model (LLM)-based minimal-edit grammatical error correction (GEC). Enforced minimal-edit constraints by introducing taxonomy-based guidelines, including a list of grammatical error rules, to limit the scope of revisions. Identified that batch processing—grouping multiple uncorrected sentences into a single input context—triggers a self-attention dilution effect, acting as a regularizer that suppresses overcorrections. Achieved a new prompt-based state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance and significantly narrowed the gap with fine-tuned models through prompt optimization utilizing Gemini 3.1-Pro. The Waymo Effect: How AI Is Quietly Reducing Research Collaboration An analysis arguing that while AI boosts individual productivity by eliminating communication friction with humans, it fuels 'decollaboration' in the research community by stripping away unexpected counterarguments and diversity of ideas. Technology that removes the inconvenience of interaction, much like Waymo autonomous driving, offers immediate benefits, but causes the loss of unexpected perspectives and the value of collective thinking that used to exist within friction. Unlike LLMs that respond at any time and follow prompts, human colleagues provide indispensable value through independent problem awareness, unprompted critique, and serendipitous discovery. Coupled with research evaluation systems centered on output and speed, the intensification of 'decollaboration'—choosing AI over humans—could undermine the diversity of ideas and research capabilities in the long run. Creativity Is the New Moat An analysis suggesting that in an era where AI makes replicating apps and designs effortless, relentless experimentation without fear of failure and creativity—much like in the Flash era—serve as the true competitive advantage and moat. While AI allows anyone to easily produce decent results, it tends to replicate the existing web, meaning that completely delegating creative decisions to AI cannot create differentiation. During the past Flash era, despite numerous inconveniences and trial-and-error, even non-experts experimented with novel interactions to uncover innovative ideas. To maintain a competitive edge, the speed provided by AI must be harnessed not for mass-producing simple clones, but for conducting more experiments and re-examining the essence of problems. Claude Is No Longer Accessible to Minors Consumer Claude has restricted its usage age to 18 and older, introducing an age verification process via the external platform Yoti for accounts suspected of belonging to minors. The consumer Claude eligibility requirement has been set to 18 and older, and accounts will be deactivated if signs of underage usage are detected. To restore an account, users must complete age verification through the third-party platform Yoti via selfie age estimation, ID verification, or the Yoti Digital ID app. Anthropic only receives pass/fail results, and Yoti deletes personal data immediately after age confirmation to protect privacy. Lean 4 Formal Proof Included in OpenAI's Navier-Stokes Announcement OpenAI presented a machine-verifiable formal proof based on Lean 4 alongside its proof of the Navier-Stokes equations, demonstrating the revolutionary potential of mathematical verification via AI. OpenAI released a machine-verifiable Lean 4 formal proof alongside the proof of the Navier-Stokes equations, an open problem in fluid dynamics. While conventional methods were estimated to require approximately 132,800 person-hours to formalize a 166-page paper, OpenAI completed the verification in Lean in just 17 hours. Beyond pure mathematics, formal verification technology can deliver high return on investment in practical domains such as security policy consistency, smart contract limit checks, and core algorithm correctness. Show GN: Release of korean-llm-v4 Announcement of the Korean-llm-v4 model, featuring a completely overhauled training architecture that systematically separates the pretraining and supervised fine-tuning (SFT) pipelines to achieve natural sentence fluency. Introduced a training pipeline that sequentially performs SFT after pretraining to overcome the limitations of relying solely on instruction datasets Applied engineering improvements including train-validation split, handling to prevent truncation of long responses, and checkpoint saving to select the optimal model state Implemented by the developer directly redesigning the training structure rather than relying on AI-generated code Training a 210M text-to-image DiT from scratch on one GPU: what I measured [P] Shares key technical findings and training recipes measured while training a 210M-parameter text-to-image Diffusion Transformer (DiT) from scratch on a single GPU environment. Learnable null slots added to cross-attention act as attention sinks, absorbing excess token mass and allowing text tokens to attend precisely to target objects. Flow-matching loss serves more as a training health signal rather than directly reflecting generative quality. Applying timestep shifts during training is more effective at improving FID and generative quality than doubling the number of inference steps. Why is TMLR so slow in recent times [D] A final-year PhD researcher expresses frustration over delays in the paper review and final acceptance process at the TMLR journal ahead of postdoctoral applications. The author submitted a single-author paper to TMLR, initially receiving positive feedback and a minor revision request. After submitting the revision, reviewer responses ceased, resulting in a processing delay of over two months. Despite sending reminders to the Action Editor and the Editor-in-Chief, the situation remains unresolved, putting the author in a difficult position with postdoctoral application deadlines approaching. Any tools to turn a codebase into a fine tuning dataset? [D] A question regarding tools and workflows for converting existing web codebases into datasets for fine-tuning coding models. Exploring ways to transform components, pages, and other assets from React/Next.js projects or static HTML sites into 'instruction-code' paired training datasets Considering strategies for preserving context across components and files, linking screenshots with code, and generating practical instructions beyond simple descriptions Aiming to build high-quality datasets and benchmarks to test a new model architecture featuring reduced VRAM usage along with improved performance and speed NeurIPS 2026: site selection email [D] A NeurIPS 2026 paper submitter received an email regarding presentation site selection and is asking the community whether this correlates with acceptance chances. A NeurIPS paper submitter received an email regarding event site selection. While the email is not an official acceptance notification, the author is asking whether it was sent to all unwithdrawn papers. The author is seeking opinions on whether receiving the email hints at higher odds of paper acceptance. CUDA/HIP: Flash Attention tuning (gfx1201) by pwilkin · Pull Request #28102 · ggml-org/llama.cpp A pull request optimizing Flash Attention performance for GPUs based on the latest AMD RDNA architecture in the llama.cpp project. Improved prompt processing (pp) speed on RDNA4 (R9700) and RDNA 3.5 (RX 9060 XT, 8060S) environments. Demonstrates superior performance metrics in large-context environments. Detailed benchmark results are included in the pull request. Fine-tuning Qwen 3 4B Base on 100 zebra puzzles yielded +31% on MATH-500. 6.5-min (Single H100/H200) reproduction notebook included. Research findings showing a 31% improvement on the MATH-500 benchmark by fine-tuning the Qwen 3 4B Base model with 100 zebra puzzle samples. Fine-tuned the Qwen 3 4B Base model using a dataset of only 100 zebra puzzles Achieved a +31% performance gain on the MATH-500 benchmark evaluation as a result of fine-tuning Provides a reproducible Jupyter notebook that runs in approximately 6.5 minutes on a single H100 or H200 GPU 7900 XTX + 32/64GB RAM for Qwen 3.8 Flash Next? A post inquiring about the performance and compatibility of running the Qwen 3.8 Flash Next model on an AMD Radeon 7900 XTX with 32/64GB RAM for local LLMs and coding agents. The author plans to build a PC for local LLMs and coding agents, considering the 7900 XTX 24GB as an alternative due to the shortage of used RTX 3090s and high prices of newer Nvidia GPUs. The author asks about user experiences regarding decode and prefill token speeds (tok/s) and the level of AMD/ROCm support when running Qwen 3.8 Flash Next on an AMD 7900 XTX. The author asks whether it is worth configuring the system with 64GB RAM versus sticking with 32GB, taking into account SSD streaming and offloading when running Q4 quantized models. Ion (zero install harness, runs on browser) A lightweight AI harness called 'Ion', which runs directly in Chromium-based web browsers without any installation, has been released. It requires no separate installation process; simply download and run the agent.html file, then connect it to an OpenAI-compatible server for immediate use. It operates solely within local folders granted access by the user, providing safety with no terminal or MCP capabilities, while enabling file editing even on mobile browsers. It provides essential tools for simple tasks, including a checkpoint feature to revert file changes and a built-in default editor. GPT Live clone on an RTX 3060 This covers an experiment implementing a GPT Live-style real-time voice assistant locally on an RTX 3060 12GB using open-source models. On an RTX 3060 with 12GB VRAM, speech recognition (Qwen3 1.7B ASR), an LLM (Qwen3.5-9B-UD-Q4_K_XL GGUF, 12K context), and speech synthesis (Pocket TTS) models were all run simultaneously. Using the custom-built Fulloch project, the setup was tested with the same complex multi-part query as the GPT Live demo (checking flight paths while simultaneously asking for destination food recommendations). Although the flight path lookup failed, restaurant recommendations were provided quickly, completing the entire process in under six and a half minutes. Claude users found ways around safeguards for bioweapons research An article regarding instances where users attempted to bypass safeguards on Anthropic's Claude model for biological weapons research but were ultimately blocked. Anthropic stated that it blocked several attempts this year to misuse its AI technology for research related to biological weapons development. Five representative cases of attempting to bypass controls and conceal research purposes were disclosed, which included users from sanctioned countries such as Russia, China, and Iran. Anthropic stated it hopes sharing these examples will spark discussions with the AI industry and governments regarding emerging biological threats and countermeasures. NVIDIA Personal AI Router Distributes AI Tasks Across Local Compute NVIDIA has released a beta version of 'NVIDIA Personal AI Router (PAIR)', which combines the inference computing power of multiple computers within a local network to distribute and process multi-agent AI requests. PAIR connects multiple devices on a local network and automatically distributes independent AI inference requests to appropriate nodes, preventing bottlenecks on a single GPU. It is compatible with existing local inference frameworks such as Ollama and LM Studio, providing a single proxy interface without requiring changes to agent harnesses or architectures. Rather than merging GPUs or pooling VRAM across multiple devices into a single unit, it allocates individual inference request units to suitable devices. How LinkedIn Trains AI Job Search 8x Faster with Multi-Teacher Distillation LinkedIn has unveiled a technical pipeline that speeds up the training of its AI job search ranking model by 8x through multi-teacher knowledge distillation and distributed training optimization. Combining SGLang-based online and offline multi-teacher distillation successfully compressed knowledge from large-scale teacher models into a lightweight 0.6B-parameter student ranking model. Achieved approximately 8x training acceleration through end-to-end system optimizations, including adopting LiGer, multi-node training, FSDP2, and leveraging H200 clusters. The 0.6B model boosted NDCG@10 by 24.48%, and improved inference throughput per GPU from 290 to over 2,000 queries per second through structured pruning and context compression. Session Traces and Cost Controls Help Diagnose AI Agent Failures Covers session tracing and cost control measures to resolve recursive calls and unexpected cost spikes that occur during AI agent operations. Traditional monitoring makes it difficult to pinpoint the root cause of agent infinite loops or incorrect endpoint calls, making nested session tracing using tools like Langfuse essential. To prevent runaway executions, proactive and real-time cost controls should be applied, such as hard iteration limits, per-tool call limits, blocking identical consecutive calls, and statistical monitoring based on moving average costs. Care must be taken to avoid high-cardinality issues caused by adding session IDs to metric labels, and systems should separate traces for debugging and metrics for alerting. PaperCut Replaces Emergency Patches With Fixes for Two Actively Exploited Flaws PaperCut has released official maintenance releases to replace previous emergency patches, addressing two security vulnerabilities that were actively exploited in the wild. PaperCut NG/MF versions 26.0.5, 25.0.13, and 24.1.10 have been released, resolving two vulnerabilities (CVE-2026-81578, CVE-2026-82078) that allow authentication bypass and arbitrary code execution. A suspected Russian-speaking threat actor leveraged hundreds of AI agents (utilizing OpenAI's Codex-based tools and DeepSeek models) to compromise more than 395 organizations across 48 countries, primarily in the U.S. education sector, on a massive scale. PaperCut strongly urged users who applied the previous emergency patches to immediately upgrade to these official maintenance releases, which have completed QA testing and incorporate additional security hardening. Cisco FMC Flaws Exploited to Steal Credentials and Deploy Qilin Ransomware Two vulnerabilities in Cisco's Secure Firewall Management Center (FMC) are being actively exploited in attacks by state-sponsored actors and cybercriminal groups to steal credentials and deploy Qilin ransomware. The maximum severity (CVSS 10.0) authentication bypass vulnerability CVE-2026-20079 and the privilege escalation and unauthorized information access flaw CVE-2026-20316 have been exploited in in-the-wild attacks. Cisco Talos identified three threat clusters (UAT-12197, UAT-11823, UAT-11988) engaging in web shell deployment, Russian-linked Cyclops Blink implant delivery, and Qilin ransomware distribution via Living off the Land (LotL) techniques. The U.S. CISA added both vulnerabilities to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog, and Cisco strongly urged customers to apply hotfixes immediately. OpenAI Launches Full-Duplex Voice Model 'GPT-Live-1' API... \"Enabling Real-Time Conversational Agents\" OpenAI has launched the 'GPT-Live-1' API, a full-duplex voice model capable of listening to human speech and responding in real time simultaneously. It integrates traditional STT, LLM, and TTS pipelines into a single model, reducing latency and supporting natural interruptions and real-time interactions. Developers can flexibly connect high-performance reasoning models, cost-effective models, or external tools behind the voice interface to build customized agents. OpenAI also unveiled 'OpenAI Presence' to support enterprise real-time voice workflows, offering 12 voices with diverse accents. FuriosaAI Establishes Singapore Subsidiary to Target the APAC Market FuriosaAI establishes a subsidiary in Singapore to target the Asia-Pacific market, centered around its second-generation AI accelerator 'RNGD'. Following Seoul, Silicon Valley, and Lisbon, FuriosaAI sets up a local subsidiary in Singapore to serve as a hub for APAC business development and technical support. Under the leadership of Addison Chi, Vice President of APAC Sales, the company plans to accelerate the infrastructure rollout of 'RNGD', its second-generation data center AI accelerator that entered mass production this January. The 'NXT RNGD' server equipped with RNGD features low-power (3kW) air-cooled deployment, and following adoption by Samsung SDS and LG AI Research, it is now pursuing global market expansion. OpenAI Introduces Enterprise Data Analysis Tool 'Data Agent' to ChatGPT Enterprise OpenAI has introduced 'Data Agent' to ChatGPT Enterprise, enabling enterprise members to analyze internal data using natural language, create interactive dashboards, and execute follow-up tasks. Enables analysis of internal structured and unstructured data and identification of root causes of changes through natural language queries without writing complex queries Supports broad integration with major data sources such as BigQuery, Snowflake, and Databricks, as well as existing BI tools including Tableau and Power BI Supports creating interactive dashboards based on analysis results, as well as suggesting follow-up tasks and executing actual actions through tool integrations Anthropic Grants EU Access to 'Mythos 5' After 4-Month Negotiation Following four months of discussions, Anthropic has provided the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity (ENISA) with access to 'Mythos 5', an AI model specialized in vulnerability detection. Following consultations between Anthropic and the European Commission (EC), the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity (ENISA) secured access to the cyber-defense AI model 'Mythos 5' and initiated live testing. Although Mythos 5 specializes in code vulnerability detection, access has been selectively granted to a limited number of organizations due to concerns over misuse and U.S. administration regulations. It is noted that this authorization is limited to an older version, while ENISA has also secured access to OpenAI's 'GPT-5.6 Cyber' and 'GPT-6 Astra'. [Bulletin Board] Synapsoft participates in 'Local Government Informatization Strategy IT Exhibition' and other briefs Covers major domestic AI companies and institutions' solution exhibitions, defense project selections, educational seminars, global collaborations, and awards. Synapsoft demonstrated five types of document AI solutions at the Local Government Informatization Strategy IT Exhibition, and Sisun AI was selected for an Air Force defense network AI coding and security inspection project valued at 2.45 billion KRW. Grep is hosting an AX education seminar for corporate HR managers, and Seoul AI Hub established a global exchange and cooperation framework for startups with Taiwan's InnoPAD Taipei. Corporate achievements followed, including CLE Robotics winning the Robotworld Award, Kolon Benit receiving a Cloudera Partner Award, and Lunit conducting joint research with 10x Genomics to identify biomarkers predicting cancer treatment response. [ZD SW Today] XL8 supplies real-time AI interpretation and translation to 'Try Everything 2026', and more An article summarizing the latest business supply and service collaboration trends of major South Korean software and AI companies, including XL8, ESTsoft, Selectstar, Synapsoft, and Douzone Bizon. XL8 supplied its real-time AI interpretation and translation solution 'EventCAT', which delivers an average latency of under 2 seconds, to more than 40 sessions at 'Try Everything 2026' and the finals of the '2026 Seoul Unicorn Challenge'. ESTsoft signed a memorandum of understanding (MOU) with K-Monster Group to expand global and domestic business for its AI human solution 'Perso AI'. Selectstar and The Pinkfong Company presented an IP-integrated 'Baby Shark' AI interactive exhibition and reliability evaluation case study at 'Try Everything 2026'. Synapsoft showcased its document-based AI platform 'INEX' at a local government IT strategy exhibition, while Douzone Bizon is running a promotion supporting its enterprise AI platform 'ONE AI' to commemorate the launch of 'DJ Bank' in collaboration with Jeju Bank."